{"agent":"fleet-testpollution","data":{"by":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":3,"detail":"The guard only ever records and compares `_config_snapshot()` (locals from `.git/config` + `core.bare`) and `git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no`; it never records `HEAD` or the branch ref. That is exactly the state a stray commit mutates, so the guard can stay silent while the item's branch is polluted with \"Test init\" commits — the headline symptom of B1d82c6b76f. Concrete input: a test that runs `git -C tmp add README.md && git -C tmp commit -m 'Test init'` while `GIT_DIR`/`GIT_WORK_TREE` still point at the real repository (i.e., any future regression of the leak this file guards against), supplying the identity with `-c user.name=… -c user.email=…` so `.git/config` is never written, and staging content that matches the work tree. After the run: `.git/config` is byte-identical to `_BASELINE` (no `user.name`, no `core.bare` change), `git status --porcelain` is identical to the baseline (clean), yet `git rev-parse HEAD` moved and `git log` shows the extra commit. `_changes(_BASELINE, snapshot(status=False))` in the module fixture and `_changes(_BASELINE, snapshot())` in `pytest_sessionfinish` both find nothing, so the suite exits 0 while the branch was rewritten — the opposite of the guarantee the guard is documented to give. (Note the per-module fixture passes `status=False` and therefore also cannot attribute tracked-file/index changes to a module; only `pytest_sessionfinish` sees them, and neither path sees refs.)","digest":"be2b35b78869dd80","location":"tests/_isolation.py `snapshot` / `pytest_sessionfinish` (and the `_real_repo_config_untouched` fixture)","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"tests/_isolation.py `snapshot` / `pytest_sessionfinish` (and the `_real_repo_config_untouched` fixture)"},{"chunk":4,"detail":"`test_a_local_identity_equal_to_the_global_one_is_not_a_finding` unconditionally reads the machine's *global* git identity via `_git(repo, \"config\", \"--global\", f\"user.{key}\")`, and `_git` runs `subprocess.run(..., check=True)`. When no global identity is configured (the normal state of a fresh CI container / isolated test runner — exactly the environment this bug is about, `pytest -n 48` under `ddflow gate run <id> ci`), `git config --global user.name` exits 1, so `subprocess.run` raises `CalledProcessError` and the test errors before exercising the code under review. The change has no setup that guarantees a global identity exists (no `git config --global …` write, no `HOME`/`GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL` isolation that would make one), it just assumes the developer's global config is present. The same assumption underlies the identity comparison: with no global value, the identity the `repo` fixture must configure locally to be able to commit will read as \"local identity unlike the global one\", so `test_a_clean_repository_has_no_hygiene_note` (line 29) also diverges. Either way the suite outcome depends on the runner's `[REDACTED:path]` rather than on the change.","digest":"e87cf89b6619f449","location":"tests/test_repo_hygiene_doctor.py:45","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"tests/test_repo_hygiene_doctor.py:45"}],"chunks_total":5,"coverage":"5/5 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":22515,"diff_sha":"bffd6562da2b3700a641998bc0929c38e8cafc42ad1462028a360c3d759d27f9","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in [REDACTED:path]","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracked":0},"elapsed_s":317.7,"family":"deepseek","findings":2,"max_chunk_chars":5000,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","output_digest":"14ed35c0f865898c","output_file":".ddflow/local/reviews/fix-B1d82c6b76f.critic.lan-deepseek-v4.1-flash.20261016T212558Z-1e4fe626.jsonl","reason":"","review_kind":"full","reviewed":[1,2,3,4,5],"reviewed_head":"ce15765c6b6e21bd194c40073a1019470718074c","reviewer":"lan-deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewer_digest":"d34b8af1b4a590b5","round":1,"rounds":1